-- Passage rg10-literary-structure-pov-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b14140a3-74a6-52ca-8677-968b3cb4f4c0', 'rg10-literary-structure-pov-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Last Four Hundred$q$, $q$With four hundred meters left in the regional cross-country final, Sun-hee Park could see the orange flags of the finish chute and the back of the runner in the green jersey, maybe fifteen steps ahead.

She had been chasing that green jersey for three miles. She had chased it up the long hill by the water tower, through the muddy stretch where the course bent around the soccer fields, and down the gravel path where her coach always stood shouting splits that she could never quite hear over her own breathing.

Now everything slowed. She noticed a paper cup tumbling across the grass. She noticed that her left shoelace had come loose and was slapping her ankle in rhythm, and that a small boy on the sideline was holding a sign with someone else's name on it, upside down. She noticed her own thoughts arriving one at a time, as calm as passengers stepping off a bus: you can catch her, you have done the work, there is still time.

Then the green jersey looked back.

Sun-hee moved. Ten steps. Six. The chute narrowed. Her arms burned. Two steps.

She crossed the line a half second behind and folded over her knees, gasping, sure she had lost. A volunteer tore the tag from her bib and pointed at the scoreboard. Sun-hee squinted. She had not beaten the green jersey. She had beaten something else: the qualifying time for the state meet, which she had missed by four seconds every race that season.

The runner in the green jersey walked over and offered a hand. "You scared me," she said. Sun-hee laughed, still unable to stand, and took it.$q$, 'draft')
